An empty logfile means no logfile.

The config's `logfile` has a compiled-in default, so there was no way to
run without one: the console (`-f`) was always a second copy of the same
lines. A supervisor that captures the console and owns the file has no
use for the copy, and an empty string is the natural way to say so.

logger_init() used to return before completing initialization when given
no file, since upstream only ever does that for the bootstrap before the
config is read. An uninitialized logger writes every message to the
console with no threshold, no domain filter and no lock, so a run with
no logfile logged at spam level whatever `loglevel` said. Initialization
now completes without a file; the bootstrap phase then honours its
threshold too (the default, or `-d`).

